<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Pellew, 1st Viscount Exmouth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lord Exmouth, Edward Pellew</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Pellew, 1st Viscount Exmouth, was a British naval officer celebrated as a frigate captain in the wars with Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, he led an Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet that bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> on 27 August 1816, destroying much of the corsair fleet and forcing the dey to free more than a thousand Christian captives and to promise an end to the enslavement of Christians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-pellew-1st-viscount-exmouth.jpg" alt="Edward Pellew, 1st Viscount Exmouth" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Northcote · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Pellew,_1st_Viscount_Exmouth_by_James_Northcote.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
